### What is the problem?

Given an m x n matrix where each row is sorted left to right and the first element of each row is greater than the last element of the previous row, determine if a target value exists in the matrix. The solution should run in O(log(m*n)) time.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class SearchMatrix {

    public static boolean searchMatrix(int[][] matrix, int target) {
        int rows = matrix.length;
        if (rows == 0) return false;
        int cols = matrix[0].length;

        int low = 0, high = rows * cols - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            int row = mid / cols;
            int col = mid % cols;
            int value = matrix[row][col];

            if (value == target) return true;
            if (value < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] ledger = {
            {10000, 20000, 30000, 40000},
            {50000, 60000, 70000, 80000},
            {90000, 100000, 110000, 120000}
        };
        System.out.println("Find 70000: " + searchMatrix(ledger, 70000));
        System.out.println("Find 55000: " + searchMatrix(ledger, 55000));
    }
}
```

```text
Find 70000: true
Find 55000: false
```

### How does the index mapping work?

The matrix has `m` rows and `n` columns. A flattened index `mid` maps to row `mid / n` and column `mid % n`. For example, in a 3x4 matrix, flattened index 6 maps to row `6 / 4 = 1` and column `6 % 4 = 2`, which is `matrix[1][2]`.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class IndexMapping {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int rows = 3, cols = 4;
        System.out.println("Flattened index -> (row, col):");
        for (int i = 0; i < rows * cols; i++) {
            System.out.println("  " + i + " -> (" + i / cols + ", " + i % cols + ")");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Flattened index -> (row, col):
  0 -> (0, 0)
  1 -> (0, 1)
  2 -> (0, 2)
  3 -> (0, 3)
  4 -> (1, 0)
  5 -> (1, 1)
  6 -> (1, 2)
  7 -> (1, 3)
  8 -> (2, 0)
  9 -> (2, 1)
  10 -> (2, 2)
  11 -> (2, 3)
```

### Why must the matrix be globally sorted?

The flattened binary search assumes reading row by row gives a non-decreasing sequence. If only each row is sorted but rows do not chain together (the first element of a row is not necessarily greater than the last of the previous row), the flattened approach gives wrong results. For such matrices, use top-right elimination instead.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class GlobalVsRowSorted {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Globally sorted: flattened binary search works
        int[][] global = {
            {10, 20, 30},
            {40, 50, 60},
            {70, 80, 90}
        };
        System.out.println("Global sorted, find 50: " + searchFlattened(global, 50));

        // Row-sorted only: flattened search may fail
        int[][] rowOnly = {
            {10, 20, 30},
            {15, 25, 35},  // 15 < 30: rows don't chain
            {28, 40, 50}
        };
        System.out.println("Row-sorted, find 25: " + searchTopRight(rowOnly, 25));
    }

    static boolean searchFlattened(int[][] m, int t) {
        int r = m.length, c = m[0].length;
        int lo = 0, hi = r * c - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int v = m[mid / c][mid % c];
            if (v == t) return true;
            if (v < t) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1;
        }
        return false;
    }

    static boolean searchTopRight(int[][] m, int t) {
        int row = 0, col = m[0].length - 1;
        while (row < m.length && col >= 0) {
            if (m[row][col] == t) return true;
            if (m[row][col] < t) row++; else col--;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

```text
Global sorted, find 50: true
Row-sorted, find 25: true
```

### What is the top-right corner elimination approach?

For matrices where each row and each column is sorted but rows do not chain together, start at the top-right corner. If the current value equals the target, return true. If it is less than the target, move down (the current row's values are all too small). If it is greater, move left (the current column's values are all too large). This eliminates one row or one column per step, giving O(m + n) time.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class TopRightElimination {

    public static boolean search(int[][] matrix, int target) {
        if (matrix.length == 0) return false;
        int row = 0, col = matrix[0].length - 1;
        while (row < matrix.length && col >= 0) {
            int val = matrix[row][col];
            if (val == target) return true;
            if (val < target) row++;    // eliminate row
            else col--;                  // eliminate column
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] ledger = {
            {1000, 4000, 7000, 11000},
            {2000, 5000, 8000, 12000},
            {3000, 6000, 9000, 16000},
            {10000, 13000, 14000, 17000}
        };
        System.out.println("Find 5000: " + search(ledger, 5000));
        System.out.println("Find 15000: " + search(ledger, 15000));
    }
}
```

```text
Find 5000: true
Find 15000: false
```

### How do the two approaches compare?

| Feature | Flattened Binary Search | Top-Right Elimination |
|---|---|---|
| Matrix type | Globally sorted | Row-sorted + column-sorted |
| Time | O(log(m * n)) | O(m + n) |
| Space | O(1) | O(1) |
| Approach | Single binary search | Walk from corner |
| Better when | Matrix is globally sorted | Matrix is only row and column sorted |

### What about the overflow-safe midpoint?

On a very large grid, `m * n` can be large. The midpoint must be computed as `low + (high - low) / 2` to avoid overflow. For a banking audit grid with millions of entries, this is not a theoretical concern.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class OverflowSafe {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int rows = 50000, cols = 50000;
        long total = (long) rows * cols; // 2.5 billion: overflows int
        System.out.println("Total cells: " + total);
        System.out.println("Fits in int? " + (total <= Integer.MAX_VALUE));
        System.out.println("Use long for high index on very large grids.");
    }
}
```

```text
Total cells: 2500000000
Fits in int? false
Use long for high index on very large grids.
```

### What are common mistakes?

The two most common mistakes:
1. Using the flattened approach on a matrix that is only row-sorted, not globally sorted.
2. Confusing the number of columns with the number of rows when computing `row = mid / cols` and `col = mid % cols`.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class MappingBug {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int rows = 3, cols = 4;
        int mid = 7;

        // CORRECT
        System.out.println("Correct: (" + mid / cols + ", " + mid % cols + ")");

        // WRONG: swapped rows and cols
        System.out.println("Wrong:   (" + mid / rows + ", " + mid % rows + ")");
    }
}
```

```text
Correct: (1, 3)
Wrong:   (2, 1)
```

### How is this used in a banking scenario?

At the National Bank of India, the end-of-day settlement report is stored as a globally sorted grid of settled amounts. The compliance officer needs an instant check for whether a specific amount has settled. The flattened binary search gives that answer in a handful of comparisons even on grids with millions of entries.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class SettlementGrid {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulate a 1000x1000 globally sorted grid
        int rows = 1000, cols = 1000;
        int[][] grid = new int[rows][cols];
        int val = 1;
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
                grid[r][c] = val++;

        int target = 500_000;
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        boolean found = searchMatrix(grid, target);
        long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
        System.out.println("Found " + target + ": " + found);
        System.out.println("Time: " + elapsed + " ns");
    }

    static boolean searchMatrix(int[][] m, int t) {
        int r = m.length, c = m[0].length;
        int lo = 0, hi = r * c - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int v = m[mid / c][mid % c];
            if (v == t) return true;
            if (v < t) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

```text
Found 500000: true
Time: 1500 ns
```
